Work in the hiyroscript/alva repository on the current main branch.

Task

Fix the visual bug where a white/light rectangular outline appears around the credits text area when the player interacts with or scrolls the credits.

The fix should be surgical. Remove the unwanted outline without changing the credits design, scrolling behavior, controls, automatic credits roll, accessibility semantics, or the rest of ALVA’s UI.

Relevant architecture and current behavior

ALVA is a vanilla HTML/CSS/JavaScript game with no UI framework. The page itself does not scroll; individual game screens and panels manage their own scrolling/interactions.

The primary affected credits implementation is the animated credits column on the Home screen.

Relevant files:

js/screens/home-screen.js

The Home screen creates a focusable credits viewport:

this.creditsViewport = el('div', {
  class: 'home-credits-col', tabindex: 0, role: 'region',
  'aria-label': 'Credits. Scroll or use arrow and Page keys to read.',
}, [this.rollTrack]);

The credits support several manual interaction methods:

* wheel/trackpad scrolling
* pointer/touch dragging
* Arrow Up / Arrow Down
* Page Up / Page Down
* Space / Shift+Space

The pointer-down handler explicitly focuses the credits viewport:

this.creditsViewport.focus({ preventScroll: true });

Do not remove this keyboard accessibility or the existing manual scrolling behavior merely to hide the visual bug.

The credits are not natively scrolling vertically on Home. Their position is driven through rollOffset and:

this.rollTrack.style.transform = `translate3d(0, ${-this.rollOffset}px, 0)`;

Automatic movement resumes after player inactivity, and reduced-motion mode uses the same input system while remaining manual-only. Preserve all of this.

Primary root cause to investigate

In styles.css, the Home credits viewport currently contains this focus styling:

.home-credits-col:focus-visible {
  outline: 2px solid var(--text);
  outline-offset: -6px;
}

--text is a very light/off-white color, so when the credits viewport becomes :focus-visible, the player sees a large light rectangular box surrounding the credits area.

This appears to be the main source of the reported white outline.

Confirm the behavior in the running game before changing it, but treat this selector as the primary suspect.

Secondary place to inspect

The standalone Help & Credits screen is implemented in:

js/screens/help-credits-screen.js

Its Help and Credits tab panels are also focusable:

tabindex: '0'

and styles.css currently includes:

.tab-panel:focus {
  outline: none;
}
.tab-panel:focus-visible {
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--border-strong);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
}

This produces another light rectangular focus treatment around a scrollable text panel.

Check whether the same reported visual artifact occurs when scrolling the Credits tab. If it does, fix that instance as part of the same change.

Do not indiscriminately remove focus styles from every component in ALVA. Buttons, tabs, menu entries, character selection, map selection, and other genuinely interactive controls should retain their existing focus treatment.

Required result

When a player scrolls, drags, or otherwise interacts with the Home credits, there must no longer be a white/light rectangular outline surrounding the credits text/viewport.

The credits should continue to look like plain text floating inside the angled glass strip. Do not introduce a new card, box, border, panel, background, or rectangular frame around them.

Keep the existing:

* angled glass treatment
* clipping and masks
* stable green slash
* typography
* credit spacing
* seamless looping roll
* manual scroll position
* automatic resume behavior
* reduced-motion behavior
* touch dragging
* wheel/trackpad support
* keyboard scrolling
* ARIA role and label
* responsive positioning

Do not change the actual credits content in js/ui/help-content.js.

Accessibility constraint

Do not solve this by globally disabling focus outlines.

In particular, do not add rules such as:

*:focus {
  outline: none;
}

and do not alter the shared focus styles used by buttons or navigation controls.

The credits region must remain keyboard-focusable because it accepts Arrow/Page/Space scrolling commands.

The goal is specifically to eliminate the large white rectangular visual frame around the credits.

If a focus indication still needs to be present for keyboard accessibility, use an ALVA-appropriate treatment that does not surround the credit text with a white rectangle. Prefer something extremely restrained and non-disruptive, such as an accent-based cue that integrates with the existing Home glass/green-slash design.

Do not add a visible treatment during ordinary mouse, touch, trackpad, or drag interaction.

If the existing :focus-visible behavior can be adjusted cleanly with CSS alone, prefer that over introducing new JavaScript modality tracking.

Implementation preference

Start with the smallest possible fix.

For the Home credits, inspect .home-credits-col:focus-visible first. If that rule alone accounts for the artifact, make the change there rather than altering the credits scrolling implementation.

Only modify js/screens/home-screen.js if CSS cannot correctly distinguish the desired interaction behavior.

For the Help & Credits .tab-panel, only change its focus treatment if you can reproduce the same unwanted light box there. Do not change tab button focus styles such as .tab:focus; those are navigation controls and are intentionally visible.

Keep selectors scoped to the affected scrolling regions.

Do not change

Do not modify unrelated gameplay or UI systems.

Specifically avoid changes to:

js/game/*

character movement or combat

stage rendering

Home credits scroll speed (ROLL_SPEED)

credits resume delay (CREDITS_RESUME_DELAY)

credit text/data in js/ui/help-content.js

Home layout geometry

glass-strip angle

clip paths or masks

green accent slash

screen transitions

global menu navigation

global focus-ring variables unless absolutely necessary

Validation

Test the final implementation with all supported credits interactions.

On the Home screen:

1. Hover over the credits and use a mouse wheel.
2. Use a trackpad to scroll.
3. Click/press and drag the credits vertically.
4. Release after dragging and confirm automatic rolling resumes after the existing delay.
5. Focus the credits using the keyboard and use Arrow Up/Down.
6. Test Page Up and Page Down.
7. Test Space and Shift+Space.
8. Confirm none of those actions creates the unwanted white/light rectangular frame.
9. Confirm the browser/page itself does not begin scrolling.
10. Confirm Play and normal menu navigation still receive their existing focus appearance.

Also test prefers-reduced-motion: reduce. The credits should remain manual-only as before and should not regain the unwanted outline.

If the Help & Credits screen can be reached during development, open the Credits tab and scroll it with the mouse/touch and keyboard. If its .tab-panel:focus-visible treatment creates the same unwanted rectangular frame, remove or redesign that treatment without affecting the tab buttons themselves.

Check both a normal desktop viewport and a short landscape/mobile-sized viewport so the fix does not interfere with clipping or responsive credits positioning.

Acceptance criteria

The change is complete when:

* No white/light rectangular outline appears around the Home credits while scrolling or dragging.
* No unwanted white/light rectangular outline appears around Credits text in the Help & Credits screen, if that screen reproduces the issue.
* The credits remain keyboard accessible.
* Arrow/Page/Space scrolling continues working.
* Wheel/trackpad scrolling continues working.
* Pointer/touch dragging continues working.
* Automatic Home credits movement still pauses and resumes exactly as before.
* Reduced-motion behavior remains unchanged.
* There is no page-level scrolling.
* There are no changes to credits content, typography, glass geometry, clipping, or the green slash.
* Existing focus indication on real buttons/menu controls remains unchanged.
* No global outline: none workaround is introduced.
* The implementation is narrowly scoped and contains no unrelated refactoring.

After making the change, summarize exactly which selectors/files were modified and explain briefly why the old focus treatment caused the visible white outline.
